              "text": "The blue hue you see on distant hills and mountains is a visual phenomenon known as **aerial perspective** (or **atmospheric perspective**). It is caused by the same scientific principle that makes the sky blue: **Rayleigh scattering**.\n\nThis effect occurs due to several factors:\n\n### 1. The Atmosphere is Full of \"Stuff\"\nWhile the air right in front of your face looks perfectly clear, the atmosphere is actually packed with trillions of tiny gas molecules (mostly nitrogen and oxygen), water vapor, and dust particles. When you look at a hill that is miles away, you aren't looking through \"empty\" space\u2014you are looking through a massive, thick column of these atmospheric particles.\n\n### 2. Rayleigh Scattering\nSunlight is made up of all the colors of the rainbow, each traveling at different wavelengths. \n* Warm colors like red, orange, and yellow have **long wavelengths** and can pass through the atmosphere relatively undisturbed. \n* Cool colors like blue and violet have **short wavelengths**. Because they are so short, they crash into the tiny gas molecules in the air and bounce around in every direction. This scattering of short-wavelength light is called **Rayleigh scattering**. \n\n### 3. The \"Blue Veil\" Effect\nWhen you look at a distant mountain, two things happen to the light reaching your eyes:\n* **The mountain\u2019s light fades:** The actual light reflecting off the mountain (the greens of the trees or the browns of the dirt) has to travel a very long distance to reach you. Along the way, much of that light gets scattered or absorbed by the air, making the mountain appear dimmer and less detailed.\n* **Blue light is added:** Sunlight hitting the air *between* you and the mountain is constantly being scattered. Because blue light scatters the most, the air itself in front of the mountain literally glows with a faint blue light. \n\nBecause the mountain is relatively dark and its own light has faded over the distance, the scattered blue light in the intervening air \"piles up\" and gets superimposed over your line of sight. It acts like a translucent blue veil draped over the landscape.\n\n---\n\n### Interesting Extras:\n* **Why artists love it:** Renaissance painters, most notably Leonardo da Vinci, realized this trick of physics. They coined the term \"aerial perspective\" and started painting distant objects bluer, hazier, and with less contrast to create a realistic 3D sense of depth on a flat canvas.\n* **Tree power (Why some mountains are extra blue):** Certain mountain ranges, like the **Blue Ridge Mountains** in the US or the **Blue Mountains** in Australia, are famously blue. While Rayleigh scattering is the main culprit, the dense forests of pine and eucalyptus trees in these regions release natural gases called *volatile organic compounds* (VOCs). These organic droplets react with the air to create a fine mist that scatters blue light even more intensely than normal air.",
